probability but airway dilation on imaging remains a strong indicator", "Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia: Congenital heart defect and chronic respiratory/sinus symptoms align with this genetic condition"], "alarm_features": ["Congenital heart defect (tricuspid stenosis)", "Persistent multisystem involvement since birth", "Airway dilation on imaging"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Cystic Fibrosis-Related Genetic Syndromes", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.7458227287816754, "posterior": 0.7458227287816754, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Primary Ciliary Dysfunction Syndromes", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.12172181033528827, "posterior": 0.12172181033528827, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Immunodeficiency Syndromes with Recurrent Infections", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.03596326214451699, "posterior": 0.03596326214451699, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease with Pulmonary Complications", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.00575412194312272, "posterior": 0.00575412194312272, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Congenital Cardiac-Pulmonary Anomalies", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.09073807679539672, "posterior": 0.09073807679539672, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2", "B3", "B4", "B5"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of chronic mucus-producing cough, airway dilation on MRI, and congenital tricuspid stenosis provide sufficient evidence to support classic cystic fibrosis (B1.1) despite preserve…", "summary": "Classic cystic fibrosis is weakened by the patient's normal growth metrics, which are incompatible with typical failure to thrive seen in CF. The cardiovascular finding (tricuspid stenosis) is not specifically linked to CF but could be part of a broader multisystem syndrome."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of chronic airway dilation and recurrent sinus infections support the diagnosis of atypical cystic fibrosis (B1.2) with preserved pancreatic function?", "summary": "Chronic airway dilation and sinus infections are consistent with atypical cystic fibrosis, but the absence of pancreatic insufficiency findings reduces specificity for this diagnosis. The evidence remains supportive but non-definitive."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of chronic sinus infections, airway dilation, and subacute progression over days support primary ciliary dysfunction syndromes (B2)?", "summary": "Chronic sinus and airway findings are typical for primary ciliary dysfunction, but the absence of situs inversus (required for Kartagener syndrome) reduces its likelihood. The subacute progression pattern does not strongly favor this diagnosis."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's normal height/weight trajectory over 10 years provide definitive evidence against classic cystic fibrosis (B1.1) despite chronic respiratory symptoms?", "summary": "Normal growth metrics definitively exclude classic cystic fibrosis, which is characterized by failure to thrive. This represents the strongest evidence against B1.1 in the differential."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of pancreatic insufficiency findings (normal weight gain, normal bowel movements) argue against atypical cystic fibrosis (B1.2) as a diagnosis?", "summary": "Preserved pancreatic function does not exclude atypical cystic fibrosis, as some variants may preserve this. The evidence is minor but does reduce diagnostic specificity for B1.2."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of situs inversus in a patient with airway dilation and chronic sinus infections definitively exclude primary ciliary dysfunction (B2) as a diagnosis?", "summary": "Absence of situs inversus definitively excludes Kartagener syndrome (a subset of primary ciliary dysfunction), but other variants of PCD without situs inversus remain possible. This reduces the posterior probability of B2 significantly."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.185], ["B1.1", 0.153], ["B2", 0.138]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.674], ["B1.1", 0.466], ["B2", 0.176]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.353], ["B1.1", 0.165], ["B1.2", 0.098]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No acute instability markers or time-critical syndromes requiring immediate action. Vital signs show mild hypotension (90/58 mmHg) and tachycardia (120/min), but no end-organ signs of shock. Oxygen saturation (94%) is borderline but not critically low. The question focuses on diagnostic associations rather than immediate life-threatening conditions.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch B1.1 (posterior 0.165) < 0.75. Dangerous alternative B1 (posterior 0.353, danger 0.8) remains unresolved with posterior ≥0.10. Tree depth is 2/3, and info_exhaustion conditions not met."}, "turn_budget_used": 4, "estimated_remaining_value": 1.0, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.1653411104323939, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following findings is associated with this patient's most likely underlying diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Diastolic murmur best heard along the right lower sternal border"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Hypocalcemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Increased chloride in the patient's sweat"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Repeat sinus infections secondary to seasonal allergies"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Social withdrawal and avoidance of eye contact"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Respiratory insufficiency due to defective ciliary clearance', 'Fibrocystic lung disease', 'Chronic pancreatitis', 'Pancreatic fibrosis', 'Immunodeficiency', 'Combined immunodeficiency', 'Chronic sinusitis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.493}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1", "label": "Cystic Fibrosis-Related Genetic Syndromes", "posterior": 0.746, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B5"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=20%, source=both]\n\nRelated to Inflammatory Bowel Disease with Pulmonary Complications: cap polyposis, chronic granulomatous disease, colitis (disease)\n\n[RAG context for 'A 10-year-old girl is admitted to the medical floor for a respiratory infection' (score=0.76):]\n  Source: [The laboratory diagnostic in patients with mucoviscidosis: A review.]. > History and Physical\n  Newborns with cystic fibrosis may be identified through screening without clinical symptoms.[18] A subgroup of newborns, however, can present with gastrointestinal symptoms such as meconium ileus or p\n[RAG context for 'A 10-year-old girl is admitted to the medical floor for a respiratory infection' (score=0.78):]\n  Source: Kartagener's syndrome: a case report. > Introduction\n  This abnormal function impairs the ability of cilia to function in a coordinated manner, impairing mucociliary clearance and causing chronic upper and lower respiratory inflammation.[5] Primary ciliar\n[RAG context for 'The patient lives in a foster home and has been admitted many times' (score=0.77):]\n  Source: Pediatrics_Nelson\n  Trypsinogen Cystic fibrosis, transient, intestinal anomalies, perinatal Repeat blood specimen, possible sweat test stress, trisomies 13 and 18, renal failure and DNA testing Creatinine phosphokinase D\n[RAG context for 'The patient lives in a foster home and has been admitted many times' (score=0.79):]\n  Source: Obstentrics_Williams\n  his disorder is caused by a mutation in the cystic ibrosis conductance transmembrane regulator (CFTR) gene, which is located on the long arm of chromosome 7 and encodes a chloride-channel protein. Alt\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  repeated episodes of pain/pressure over frontal sinuses and chronic mucus-producing cough since birth → mucociliary clearance defects → Primary Ciliary Dysfunction Syndromes\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for primary ciliary dyskinesia with nasal nitric oxide testing and ciliary ultrastructure analysis\n  chronic respiratory symptoms with growth parameters in 25th percentile since birth → pancreatic insufficiency → Cystic Fibrosis-Related Genetic Syndromes\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Order sweat chloride testing and CFTR mutation analysis to confirm cystic fibrosis\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
